4. CORE DAA CONCEPT: GRAPH COLORING

Implement and explain this concept throughout the application.

In graph coloring:

A vertex represents one individual class-session event that needs to be scheduled.

An edge connects two events that must not occur at the same time.

A color represents a timetable time slot, such as Monday, Period 1.

Adjacent vertices must not receive the same time-slot color.

Each event must be assigned a valid slot that satisfies the academic constraints.

Important: represent individual weekly meetings as separate vertices. For example, three weekly Mathematics lectures for CSE-A are three different class-session events, not one vertex.

Create an edge between two events when they share a student section or cohort, share a teacher, or have another direct mutual-exclusion constraint.

Classrooms introduce additional resource constraints. The room allocator must ensure that a room is used by at most one event at any overlapping time and that the room is appropriate for the event.

The timetable slots form a predefined set of available colors. Because practical scheduling also includes availability and room constraints, do not claim that a heuristic always finds a mathematically optimal timetable or the minimum possible number of colors.

Include a dedicated Graph Coloring Explained section for students who are learning the concept.

Explain graph coloring using a small example:

Events:

E1: Mathematics for CSE-A.

E2: Data Structures for CSE-A.

E3: Mathematics for CSE-B.

E4: Operating Systems for CSE-B.

Illustrate conflicts based on shared sections or teachers. Explain how events connected by an edge cannot receive the same time slot, whereas compatible events can run simultaneously in separate rooms.

Display the corresponding graph, its vertices, its edges, the adjacency list, and the assigned time slots.

