9. TIMETABLE GENERATION ENGINE

This is the most important feature of the entire project.

Implement the scheduling engine in real TypeScript code. Separate the algorithm from the UI.

Use a clear internal model for:

Sections.

Teachers.

Subjects.

Rooms.

Class-session events.

Time slots.

Conflict graph vertices.

Conflict graph edges.

Generated timetable entries.

Validation results.

Each class-session event must have a unique ID, subject, teacher, participating section IDs, duration, required room type, enrollment, and scheduling preferences.

Build the timetable in the following stages.

Stage 1: Validate the inputs

Check for missing teachers, invalid room capacities, incomplete subject assignments, impossible durations, missing calendar slots, and contradictory restrictions.

Explain input errors clearly before starting the algorithm.

Stage 2: Expand weekly sessions

Convert every subject's weekly session requirement into individual events.

For example, a subject requiring three sessions per week should generate three separate event vertices.

Stage 3: Construct the conflict graph

Build an adjacency-list representation of the conflict graph.

Add an edge when two events share a teacher or at least one student section, or when another explicitly configured pairwise hard constraint prohibits simultaneous scheduling.

Do not add an edge merely because two subjects are different.

Ensure the graph is undirected and has no duplicate edges or self-loops.

Display the number of vertices, edges, and each event's degree.

Stage 4: Generate candidate time slots

Create the list of valid timetable time slots from the academic calendar.

Exclude breaks, periods outside the working calendar, and slots unavailable to the participating sections or teachers.

For a laboratory session, generate only candidate start times that provide the required number of consecutive periods without crossing breaks or the end of the day.

Stage 5: Color the graph

Use the selected graph-coloring algorithm to determine the time assignment of each event.

A slot can be reused by nonconflicting events, provided sufficient compatible rooms are available.

Use the graph-coloring constraints together with a resource-aware room allocator.

Stage 6: Allocate rooms

For each scheduled event, identify compatible rooms that have sufficient capacity and are available throughout the event's duration.

When several compatible rooms exist, choose one that does not conflict with already scheduled events.

If the algorithm cannot allocate a room, try another candidate slot or backtrack.

Room allocation must be integrated with scheduling rather than performed through a final step that silently creates room conflicts.

Stage 7: Improve the schedule

Among valid solutions, prefer timetables that:

Distribute subject sessions across different days.

Respect preferred time slots.

Avoid excessive gaps where practical.

Balance daily teaching loads.

Avoid scheduling the same subject too many times on one day.

Use rooms efficiently.

Track soft-constraint violations separately from hard-constraint violations.

Stage 8: Independently verify the result

Run a separate timetable validator after generation.

Verify every hard constraint before declaring success.

If the schedule is incomplete, clearly display the unscheduled events and likely reasons, such as unavailable teachers, insufficient compatible rooms, or restrictive time slots.

Never silently discard a required session or report an incomplete schedule as a complete success.

10. GRAPH COLORING ALGORITHMS

Implement three scheduling methods.

Algorithm A: Greedy Graph Coloring

Use a deterministic greedy method with vertices ordered by descending degree, with stable tie-breaking.

For each vertex, assign the earliest feasible timetable slot that does not conflict with already assigned adjacent vertices and that satisfies availability and room constraints.

Explain the first-fit coloring idea.

Display the resulting timetable, assigned slots, number of sessions scheduled, and validation results.

Algorithm B: DSATUR Algorithm

Make DSATUR the default recommended algorithm.

DSATUR means Degree of Saturation.

At each scheduling step:

Select an uncolored vertex with the highest saturation degree.

Saturation degree is the number of distinct time-slot colors already assigned to its colored neighbors.

If saturation degrees are equal, select the vertex with the highest graph degree.

Apply additional stable tie-breakers, such as event duration and event ID.

Find a feasible time slot that respects the graph, availability, duration, and room constraints.

Assign that slot and update neighboring saturation information.

Repeat until every event is scheduled or no feasible assignment can be found.

Try alternative candidate slots whenever room allocation or additional constraints prevent the first candidate from working.

Use backtracking when appropriate to reconsider previous assignments.

Explain why DSATUR often performs well on practical graph-coloring problems, but do not guarantee that it always produces the optimal solution.

Algorithm C: Backtracking Graph Coloring

Implement a backtracking scheduler for smaller datasets.

The algorithm should:

Select an unassigned event.

Try each feasible time slot.

Check graph-coloring and resource constraints.

Continue recursively if the assignment is valid.

Backtrack when a later event cannot be assigned.

Stop after finding a complete valid solution or reaching a documented search limit.

Use forward checking and a configurable node or search limit to prevent extremely long runs.

If the search limit is reached, clearly indicate that the search was stopped and that optimality or infeasibility has not been proven.

Never label a failed search as proof that no valid timetable exists.

